[cs012105@cs.brown.edu (Ryan Martin) writes:]
[] What do thrones do besides this?  I have _never_ had a throne do anything
[] besides telling me that I feel out of place and then vanishing in a puff of
[] logic. After taking on all the heavyweights in a throne room, this is not
[] much of a reward.

hmm, case in point!  with that kind of history you'd have to be
precognitive or something to continue with that practice.

to my tastes, the big feature here is what the throne may or may not do.
the big flaw is that you'll never discover this feature (on average)
without a cheat.  it is assumed that the feature is self-regulated
against abuse, so how does it hurt the enjoyment of the game to advertise
these (all too subtle) areas.  using these "hidden" features enhances
the enjoyment of the game, to me, whereas not knowing about them makes
the game seem less desirable.

let me say it another way: the point should be that players should be
sitting in thrones with the expectation that something exotic may happen
and not that players should have to decipher that sitting in thrones
may in fact cause exotic things to happen.  you can only take the latter
course if you provide _adequate_ clues and more favorable circumstances.
this puts newcomers and old addicts on an even par and doesn't change
the fact that good strategy is what gets a win (not insider info!).

now, you wanted to know what could happen at a throne.  well ... various
things, both good and bad depending on your game history.  you'll just
have to sit down and experience this for yourself (now that you _know_
that potentially thrones can be very magical indeed!).   geez, what
a dirty guy i am ...

